Neuro-Tactile Synesthesia: A Unified Visuo‑Tactile Framework for General‑Purpose Dexterous Manipulation of Deformable Objects

ouadi Maakoul 

Abstract

Robots still fail when handling soft, fragile, or contact‑rich materials because they lack a physically grounded, anticipatory fusion of vision and touch. We introduce the Visuo‑Tactile Synesthesia Model (VTSM), a unified framework that learns a shared latent representation between vision and touch, endowing a robot with “tactile imagination”: it can hallucinate tactile feedback from visual context before physical contact, and use this prediction to proactively adjust its manipulation strategy. The architecture is built on three interdependent pillars: (1) a multimodal manifold alignment trained with domain‑adaptation to bridge the sim‑to‑real gap in tactile sensing; (2) an online material identification module that estimates an object’s physical parameters with exponential convergence, governed by an efficient persistent excitation monitor that balances the need for probing against task completion; and (3) a safety‑biased diffusion policy, distilled via consistency models to meet the real‑time control budget, and protected by a chattering‑resistant residual monitor and a hard 1 kHz impedance reflex. The system is evaluated on three stringent benchmark tasks—in‑hand cable threading, impedance‑controlled cloth folding, and precision grasping of delicate fruit—chosen not as an exhaustive proof but as exemplars that stress the core innovations. The proposal goes beyond a mere system integration: it formalises the Dexterity Collapse failure mode, explaining how each component disrupts the cascade from perceptual uncertainty to catastrophic physical damage. Moreover, we argue that the principles—anticipatory cross‑modal perception, verified parameter identification, layered safety—constitute a general‑purpose foundation for soft‑body manipulation, even if the immediate implementation is task‑specific. We outline a path toward broader applicability via modular fine‑tuning, few‑shot context adaptation, and future hardware validation. Ethical implications and societal impact are discussed, and all hardware and algorithmic components are specified down to the pseudo‑code level. The proposal delivers a complete mathematical, architectural, and experimental blueprint for a PhD that advances safe, flexible, and ultimately general‑purpose dexterous manipulation.

