1. Introduction & Motivation

1.1 The Dexterity Collapse

Humans can gently fold a silk scarf, route a stiff cable through a narrow clip, or pick a ripe strawberry without bruising it—all with seamless integration of vision and touch. Robots, in contrast, still treat these materials as anomalies. When a vision‑based planner misestimates a cable’s stiffness by a fraction, the resulting contact forces spike, the cable buckles, and the task fails catastrophically: the cable tangles, the fabric tears, or the strawberry is crushed. We call this failure mode Dexterity Collapse. It is not a simple error; it is a cascade in which a small perceptual misjudgement rapidly amplifies into a large, irreversible physical outcome because the controller lacks an internal model of the material’s compliance and the ability to anticipate contact forces.

Quantitative definition. We define Dexterity Collapse as a manipulation episode in which (i) the robot’s predicted contact force distribution, based on its current model of the object, deviates from the actual measured forces by more than a threshold \epsilon_{\text{DC}} for a sustained interval T_{\text{DC}}, and (ii) this mismatch results in either task failure or irreversible damage to the object. This measurable definition allows us to track and test the effectiveness of the proposed countermeasures.

1.2 The Synesthetic Solution

Our central hypothesis is that the key to preventing Dexterity Collapse lies in anticipatory cross‑modal perception: a robot should be able to “feel” an object before touching it, by predicting tactile feedback from visual observations and a brief, safe interaction probe. VTSM realises this idea as a tightly integrated system:

· Synesthetic Perception (Work Package 1) directly addresses the perceptual uncertainty at the root of Dexterity Collapse. By learning a shared latent space that translates vision into expected touch, the robot can pre‑tune its impedance and approach with an appropriate force profile, rather than reacting after damage has occurred.
· Online Material Identification (Work Package 2) attacks the unmodeled material compliance trigger. By executing a PE‑verified probe, the robot rapidly identifies the true stiffness, damping, and friction of the object, populating a digital twin that enables long‑horizon planning under accurate dynamics.
· Safety‑Biased Generative Control (Work Package 3) provides a two‑layer defence: a probabilistic bias in the diffusion policy that steers actions away from force‑limit violations, and a deterministic 1 kHz reflex that guarantees no force limit is ever exceeded, no matter what the policy generates.

Each component is necessary, but none alone is sufficient. Their combination breaks the Dexterity Collapse cascade at multiple points—perception, dynamics, and actuation—yielding a system that is robust to the very uncertainties that paralyse current approaches.

1.3 Scope: Toward a General‑Purpose Framework

We acknowledge that “general‑purpose dexterous manipulation” is an ambitious, long‑horizon goal. The three benchmark tasks we have chosen—cable threading, cloth folding, strawberry grasping—are not intended as an exhaustive proof of full generality. Rather, they are exemplars: canonical challenges that each stress a critical component of the VTSM architecture. Success on these tasks demonstrates that the underlying principles are sound and that the framework can be extended to new objects and tasks. We outline a concrete path toward broader applicability: because the synesthetic encoder and the diffusion policy operate on a latent representation that is task‑agnostic, adapting to a novel task requires only a small amount of new demonstration data and fine‑tuning of lightweight policy heads, while the core perception and safety layers remain unchanged. This modular design is the hallmark of a foundation model, even if the full validation of cross‑task transfer remains for future work.

