2. State of the Art & The Delta

(We retain the updated 2026 context from the Final Submission Version, highlighting the shift to flow matching, tactile foundation models, and differentiable simulation, and we re‑articulate the unique delta.)

Deformable Object Manipulation (DOM). Deep RL in SoftGym and DexoraSim has advanced cloth folding and cable routing, but policies overfit to training materials. Domain randomization yields conservative behaviours that cannot handle the long tail of real‑world material variations.

Visuo‑Tactile Sensing and Sim‑to‑Real Gap. Optical tactile sensors (GelSight, DIGIT) provide high‑resolution stress maps. The TACTO simulator renders plausible tactile images but fails to capture shear nonlinearities, elastomer ageing, and complex real‑world noise. No prior manipulation policy has explicitly used adversarial domain adaptation to align simulated and real tactile latent spaces, which is a core contribution of WP1.

Diffusion Policies and Accelerated Inference. Diffusion Policy (Chi et al., 2023) and extensions produce multimodal action distributions. Recent work on consistency models (Song et al., 2023) enables single‑ or few‑step generation, but their combination with safety gradients and hardware reflex layers is unexplored.

Differentiable Simulation for System ID. DiffTaichi and MuJoCo XLA enable gradient‑based material parameter estimation, but they lack online PE verification and probing‑task trade‑off mechanisms. VTSM’s efficient PE monitor and probing budget constitute a novel online identification loop.

The Delta. No prior system integrates (i) a synesthetic latent space with tactile domain adaptation, (ii) online material ID with verified exponential convergence under a computationally efficient PE monitor and task‑aware probing budget, (iii) a safety‑biased, real‑time diffusion policy with chattering‑free fallback, and (iv) a formalised Dexterity Collapse framework that maps each module to a specific failure mechanism. Removing any one of these components breaks the closed‑loop robustness, a claim we test through rigorous ablation.

