4. Research Methodology and Mathematical Framework

We present the three Work Packages (WPs) with full theoretical foundations, addressing the hardware and generalisation concerns raised.

4.1 Work Package 1: Synesthesia Encoder with Tactile Domain Adaptation

Objective. Learn an encoder pair E_v, E_t mapping to a common latent space \mathcal{Z} where vision can predict touch, preserving the local manifold structure (Proposition 1) and remaining robust to sensor imperfections.

Mathematical Formulation.
Given paired visual frames v \in \mathcal{V} (RGB‑D point clouds) and tactile frames t \in \mathcal{T} (stress maps), we minimize:

\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{recon}} + \lambda_{\text{align}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{align}} + \lambda_{\text{dom}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{dom}},

with \mathcal{L}_{\text{recon}} the cross‑modal masked reconstruction loss, \mathcal{L}_{\text{align}} the InfoNCE contrastive loss, and \mathcal{L}_{\text{dom}} the domain adversarial loss for sim‑to‑real alignment.

Addressing Sensor Noise and Drift.
The tactile encoder E_t includes an uncertainty head that outputs per‑pixel aleatoric uncertainty \sigma_t. This uncertainty is trained to increase for saturated pixels and for regions with high noise variance. During online operation, if the measured pixel noise (estimated via high‑pass filtering) exceeds expected levels—indicating sensor degradation or external vibration—the system inflates \sigma_t globally, effectively down‑weighting the tactile latent in the policy conditioning. Additionally, a low‑frequency recalibration routine runs when the robot is idle: the GelSight sensor is pressed against a flat rigid surface, and the bias/drift in the baseline intensity is estimated and compensated in the tactile pre‑processing pipeline.

Robustness to Mechanical Imperfections.
The encoder is trained with randomised small affine perturbations of the tactile images that simulate the effects of slight misalignments or backlash in the gripper mounting (e.g., translation <1 mm, rotation <2°). This makes the embedding robust to the typical pose errors present in physical LEAP‑Hand/GelSight assemblies.

Proposition 1 (Local Isometry under Uncertainty).
Under the stated loss and noise augmentation, the composed map E_t^{-1} \circ E_v is locally bi‑Lipschitz on the unsaturated sensory domain, with distortion bounded by the product of Lipschitz constants of the decoders. The proof follows from the alignment–uniformity analysis of contrastive learning and the regularisation effect of denoising reconstruction.

