4.2 Work Package 2: Efficient Online Material Identification with PE‑Task Trade‑Off

Objective. Identify the material parameter vector \theta of an unknown soft object via a single interactive probe, with provable convergence and minimal disruption to the ongoing task.

Mathematical Formulation.
We assume a linearly parameterized internal force model \mathbf{f}_{\text{int}} = \Psi(\mathbf{q}_d)\theta. The estimator follows the normalized gradient law:

\dot{\hat{\theta}} = -\Gamma\frac{\Psi^\top(\Psi\hat{\theta} - y)}{1+\|\Psi\|_F^2}.

Theorem 2 (Exponential Convergence under PE).
If the probe trajectory ensures that \Psi(t) is persistently exciting, then the parameter error \tilde{\theta} converges to zero exponentially. The proof (see Appendices) uses the Lyapunov function V = \frac12\tilde{\theta}^\top\Gamma^{-1}\tilde{\theta} and the fact that the time‑varying matrix P(t) = \Gamma\frac{\Psi^\top\Psi}{1+\|\Psi\|_F^2} inherits the PE property, leading to exponential stability of \dot{\tilde{\theta}} = -P(t)\tilde{\theta}.

Efficient PE Monitoring under Computation and Time Constraints.
To avoid a full eigenvalue decomposition at every step, we maintain a low‑rank approximation of the information matrix via online incremental SVD (iSVD) of the regressor stream. The smallest singular value of this low‑rank approximation serves as a proxy for \lambda_{\min}. The monitor runs at 20 Hz (every 5 control cycles) and consumes <5% of a Jetson Orion CPU core thanks to the iSVD’s O(k^2) per‑update cost.

To prevent the probing from consuming excessive task time, we allocate a probing budget—a hard limit on the total time spent in probing actions (e.g., 10% of the overall task duration). If the PE condition is not met within the budget, the system aborts the identification, marks \hat{\theta} as low‑confidence, and switches to a conservative “high‑stiffness” policy mode. This ensures that the theoretical convergence guarantee is only invoked when its preconditions are satisfied, and that task completion is never indefinitely halted by identification attempts.

Handling Hardware‑Induced Errors in Force Measurement.
The measured contact force y is filtered by a low‑pass Butterworth filter (cut‑off 50 Hz) to remove high‑frequency noise and avoid corrupting the regressor. A bias estimator using rest‑contact periods compensates for sensor drift. These simple but necessary steps are explicitly integrated into the identification pipeline.

Pseudo‑code (full version in Appendices) demonstrates the online SVD, budget check, and fallback logic.

