4.3 Work Package 3: Safety‑Biased Diffusion Policy with Chattering‑Free Fallback

Objective. Generate dexterous action chunks that respect force limits, with a hard safety guarantee independent of the generative model.

Mathematical Formulation.
The policy is a consistency‑distilled diffusion model (single‑step or 2‑step) that samples from the tilted distribution:

p_{\text{safe}}(\mathbf{a}|\mathbf{z}) \propto p_\theta(\mathbf{a}|\mathbf{z})\,e^{-\lambda J_{\text{safe}}(\mathbf{a})},

where J_{\text{safe}} penalises predicted contact forces exceeding \tau_{\max}. Theorem 3 (probabilistic bias) is unchanged from previous versions.

Chattering‑Free Residual Monitor.
To distinguish transient force mismatches from genuine model failure, we implement an energy‑based smoothing with hysteresis. The averaged residual R_{\text{avg}}(t) is computed via EMA over 40 ms. The system enters passive admittance mode only when R_{\text{avg}} exceeds a high threshold \epsilon_{\text{high}}, and exits only after R_{\text{avg}} has remained below a low threshold \epsilon_{\text{low}} for a minimum of 100 ms. If more than three mode switches occur within 1 s, the system locks in passive mode and requests human intervention, preventing chattering oscillations.

Hard 1 kHz Reflex.
The impedance reflex on the Jetson Orin overrides any action that would cause the real‑time force reading to exceed the limit. The reflex is implemented with a simple admittance law and runs on a real‑time core, ensuring <1 ms latency. This provides the deterministic guarantee that no force limit is ever violated, irrespective of the policy’s probabilistic bias.

Handling Latency and Synchronisation.
To mitigate jitter and missed deadlines, a dedicated PolicyBuffer stores the latest action chunk. If a new chunk is not ready by the 30 Hz tick, the controller linearly interpolates the remaining chunk, guaranteeing smooth motion. The communication between the workstation GPU and the Jetson uses ROS 2 DDS with Real‑Time QOS, and all sensor timestamps are aligned via a shared NTP‑synchronised clock in simulation (simulated time), ensuring that the residual monitor receives temporally consistent data.

Pseudo‑code for the real‑time loop with hysteresis is provided in the Appendices.

