6. Validation Protocol & Benchmark Tasks

6.1 Simulation Environment and Fidelity

We use Isaac Sim 2024.2 with the TACTO plugin for tactile rendering, and MuJoCo XLA for soft‑body dynamics and differentiable FEM. To close the sim‑to‑real gap, the simulation is configured with:

· A detailed UR5e model including joint friction and backlash emulation (±0.2°).
· GelSight simulation with elastomer nonlinear stress‑strain curve, pixel saturation, and add‑on structured noise as described in WP1.
· ROS 2 simulated time with NTP synchronisation among nodes.

Although no physical experiments are included in the PhD plan, we outline a clear future hardware deployment path: after simulation success, the trained models will be transferred to the physical setup with a short (days) fine‑tuning phase on real data. The proposal includes a dedicated section on simulation‑to‑real bridging strategies (domain adversarial training, noise injection, and sensor calibration routines) that are designed to minimise the gap.

6.2 Benchmark Tasks

The three tasks are chosen as exemplars that isolate specific components of VTSM:

· Task A: In‑hand Cable Threading (DLO). The robot must re‑orient a 30 cm flexible cable and thread its tip through a 1 cm hole. The cable stiffness ranges from very soft (silicone) to stiff (solid core).
    Primary stress: Synesthetic anticipation and online material ID. Without hallucination, the robot cannot pre‑shape the thread motion; without ID, the bending model is wrong and the cable snags.
    Metrics: success rate, material ID error, number of reflex triggers, completion time.
· Task B: Impedance‑Controlled Cloth Folding (DSO). Fold a 30×30 cm cloth square into quarters. The cloth type (cotton vs. silk) is unknown.
    Primary stress: Probing‑task trade‑off. The robot cannot repeatedly probe mid‑fold without creasing the cloth, so the probing budget must be carefully managed.
    Metrics: folding IoU, probing budget used, chattering count, reflex triggers.
· Task C: Precision Grasping of Strawberry Analogs (Soft Food). Pick a simulated strawberry from a cluttered bin without exceeding a 2 N force limit. The berry is non‑uniformly soft and saturates the GelSight on contact.
    Primary stress: Safety layer and hallucination under saturation. If the synesthetic model cannot handle saturation, the policy will mispredict forces and trigger the reflex excessively, risking drops.
    Metrics: unmitigated force violations (must be zero), success rate (berry intact), reflex frequency.

6.3 Ablation Study

All combinations tested with 30 random material seeds per task:

