Hypotheses are that each ablation drastically reduces performance on its primary stress task, proving the co‑necessity of the components.

6.4 Extending Beyond the Exemplars – Path to Generality

We do not claim that success on three tasks proves full generality. Instead, we argue that the architecture is designed for extensibility. The latent space \mathcal{Z} is task‑agnostic; the diffusion policy is conditioned on a goal embedding that can be produced by a simple visual goal encoder for new tasks. To adapt VTSM to a new manipulation (e.g., dough rolling), one would need only a few dozen teleoperated demonstrations to fine‑tune the policy head and, if the material is novel, a single probe to update the material ID. We will explicitly test this few‑shot adaptability in a supplementary experiment: after the main training, we introduce a fourth hold‑out task (e.g., folding a paper airplane) with only 10 demonstrations, and measure the success rate after fine‑tuning. This experiment demonstrates the “foundation model” character of the framework.

