Appendix A – Mathematical Proofs and Derivations

A.1 Formal Definition of Dexterity Collapse

Definition (Dexterity Collapse). A manipulation episode \mathcal{E} is said to suffer Dexterity Collapse if, during a continuous interval I = [t_0, t_0 + \Delta t] with \Delta t \ge T_{\text{DC}}, the following two conditions hold simultaneously:

1. Sustained model mismatch: The normalised force prediction error exceeds a threshold \epsilon_{\text{DC}}:
   \frac{\|\mathbf{f}_{\text{real}}(t) - \mathbf{f}_{\text{pred}}(t)\|}{\|\mathbf{f}_{\text{real}}(t)\| + \delta} > \epsilon_{\text{DC}}, \quad \forall t \in I,
   where \delta > 0 avoids division by zero.
2. Irreversible task damage: The episode ends in either (a) task failure (as defined per benchmark) or (b) permanent material damage (tearing, plastic deformation, bruising) that would render the object unusable for its intended purpose.

This definition makes Dexterity Collapse a measurable event, enabling direct evaluation of the system’s robustness.

---

A.2 Hybrid Rigid‑Deformable Dynamics (Preliminaries)

The coupled dynamics are as described in the main proposal. The stiffness of the FEM system and the contact constraints create a high‑dimensional, stiff hybrid system intractable for real‑time MPC. VTSM circumvents this via (i) learned latent dynamics (WP1), (ii) reduced‑order digital twin (WP2), and (iii) layered control (WP3).

---

A.3 Proof of Proposition 1: Local Isometry with Uncertainty

Statement. Under the combined masked reconstruction, InfoNCE contrastive, and domain adversarial losses, the encoders E_v, E_t satisfy: for any two points s_1, s_2 in the contact manifold \mathcal{M} whose tactile observations are not saturated, there exist constants 0 < c_1 \le c_2 such that

c_1\, d_{\mathcal{M}}(s_1,s_2) \le \|z(s_1) - z(s_2)\| \le c_2\, d_{\mathcal{M}}(s_1,s_2),

where z = E_v(v) or E_t(t). In saturated regions, the latent representation’s variance increases, reflecting the loss of injectivity.

Proof sketch.

· The reconstruction loss ensures that D_v \circ E_v \approx \text{id} on the data manifold, hence E_v is injective on its image, with a smooth inverse given by the decoder. The same holds for E_t.
· The InfoNCE contrastive term forces embeddings of corresponding visual and tactile observations to be close (alignment) while keeping all embeddings uniformly spread on the hypersphere (uniformity). This regularises the embedding space to be locally isometric to the data manifold.
· The adversarial domain loss aligns the distributions of simulated and real tactile latent codes, so the bi‑Lipschitz property learned in simulation transfers to the real domain after fine‑tuning.
· Saturation pixels are masked in the reconstruction loss and assigned high uncertainty; thus the encoder’s Jacobian in those dimensions shrinks, and the corresponding latent dimensions are inflated in variance. The bi‑Lipschitz bounds degrade gracefully: the effective distortion in saturated regions is multiplied by the ratio of unsaturated to saturated pixels.

---

A.4 Proof of Theorem 2: Exponential Convergence under Verified PE

Statement. Assume the internal force is linearly parameterized: \mathbf{f}_{\text{int}}(\mathbf{q}_d,\theta) = \Psi(\mathbf{q}_d)\theta. The estimator follows:

\dot{\hat{\theta}} = -\Gamma\frac{\Psi^\top(\Psi\hat{\theta} - y)}{1 + \|\Psi\|_F^2}.

If the trajectory of \Psi(t) is persistently exciting (PE), i.e., \exists \alpha,T > 0 such that \int_t^{t+T} \Psi(\tau)^\top\Psi(\tau) d\tau \succeq \alpha I for all t, then \tilde{\theta} = \hat{\theta} - \theta^* \to 0 exponentially.

Proof. (Expanded from V5.0 with details on low‑rank approximation justification.)
Define V = \frac12 \tilde{\theta}^\top \Gamma^{-1} \tilde{\theta}. Its derivative along the estimator dynamics is:

\dot{V} = -\tilde{\theta}^\top \frac{\Psi^\top\Psi}{1 + \|\Psi\|_F^2} \tilde{\theta} \le 0.

Since \Psi is PE and bounded, the matrix P(t) = \Gamma \frac{\Psi^\top\Psi}{1+\|\Psi\|_F^2} is also PE. The homogeneous time‑varying linear system \dot{\tilde{\theta}} = -P(t)\tilde{\theta} is therefore globally exponentially stable (Narendra & Annaswamy, 2005, Theorem 2.16). Hence, \|\tilde{\theta}(t)\| \le \kappa e^{-\gamma t}\|\tilde{\theta}(0)\|.

To make PE verification computationally efficient, we use an incremental SVD (iSVD) maintaining a rank‑k approximation of the regressor stack. The smallest singular value of this approximation, \sigma_{\min}^{(k)}, is a lower bound on the true smallest singular value up to the truncation error. If \sigma_{\min}^{(k)} \ge \sqrt{\alpha} over windows of length T, then PE holds with probability close to one. This avoids full eigenvalue decomposition and enables a 20 Hz monitor on the Jetson Orin.

---

A.5 Proof of Theorem 3: Probabilistic Safety Bias

Statement. Modifying the reverse SDE score by adding \lambda \nabla_{\mathbf{a}} J_{\text{safe}}(\mathbf{a}) yields a stationary distribution proportional to p_\theta(\mathbf{a}|\mathbf{z}) e^{-\lambda J_{\text{safe}}(\mathbf{a})}. Consequently, \mathbb{E}_{p_{\text{safe}}}[J_{\text{safe}}] < \mathbb{E}_{p_\theta}[J_{\text{safe}}] for any \lambda > 0 provided J_{\text{safe}} is not constant. Deterministic safety is provided separately by the 1 kHz reflex.

Proof. (Song et al., 2021; Dhariwal & Nichol, 2021). The guided reverse SDE corresponds to a Langevin dynamics with potential -\log p_\theta - \lambda J_{\text{safe}}, whose invariant measure is the tilted distribution. The exponential tilting suppresses regions of high cost.

