Appendix F – Ethical and Societal Impact (Extended Discussion)

The ability to handle soft, fragile objects with high dexterity has transformative potential. In food processing, it could reduce waste and improve hygiene. In healthcare, robots could assist with dressing or handle delicate tissues during surgery. However, the displacement of manual workers in sectors like garment manufacturing must be managed through policy and retraining.

The safety mechanisms built into VTSM (reflex layer, residual monitor) make the robot inherently safe for human‑proximate operation, an advantage over purely learning‑based systems. We have also begun investigating the energy passivity of the impedance controller to further ensure collision safety.

While any advanced technology can be misused, the nature of this research—focused on gentle, force‑limited interaction—is inherently safety‑oriented. We advocate for open publication and the development of clear guidelines for deploying dexterous robots in shared environments.

